===Etymology===
Possible alteration of {{m|en|high-fluting}}. In his book, ''The Adventure of English'', Melvyn Bragg makes the unreferenced statement that in a nascent America, when the well-to-do travelled by steamboat, said passengers were referred to as highfalutin due to the high fluted funnels on the boats.<ref>{{cite-book
|year=2003
|author=Melvyn Bragg
|authorlink=Melvyn Bragg
|title=The Adventure of English
|chapter=Wild West Words
|page=178
|publisher=Sceptre
|passage=The poor travelled  on rafts which they steered with oars called ‘riffs’ – the ‘riff-raff’ {{...}}. On board the bigger boats the richer travellers were called ‘highfalutin’ because of the high fluted smokestacks that carried the soot and cinders well away from the passengers. And they gambled.}}</ref> Compare {{m|en|riffraff}}. Another speculation connects the term with {{m|en|[[high]]-[[flighting]]/-[[flying]]}}.
|Also worth considering is the Dutch word hoogverheven meaning lofty or exalted.

